     wwaattcchhddooggdd — watchdog timer retrigger daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     wwaattcchhddooggdd [--ddnnqq] [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l] [--pp _p_e_r_i_o_d]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     wwaattcchhddooggdd is a daemon to activate and periodically retrigger the
     watchdog(4) timer device from userland.  wwaattcchhddooggdd is designed to work in
     high load environments, where other methods (such as a shell script in‐
     voking sysctl(8)) would involve too much overhead.

     The basic premise is that after every _i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l seconds, wwaattcchhddooggdd resets
     the hardware timer to _p_e_r_i_o_d.  See also watchdog(4) for more information
     on how watchdog timers work.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, wwaattcchhddooggdd will
             run in the foreground.

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l
             Specify how often, in seconds, wwaattcchhddooggdd should retrigger the
             hardware timer.  If no interval is specified, the value of _p_e_r_i_o_d
             (see below) divided by three is used.

     --nn      Do not restore the watchdog to its original values once it has
             been activated.  With this set, the system will be rebooted by
             the watchdog even after a halt(8).

     --pp _p_e_r_i_o_d
             Set the hardware timer to expire in _p_e_r_i_o_d seconds.  The default
             is 30 seconds.

     --qq      Be quiet.  With this option specified, wwaattcchhddooggdd will not output
             a warning message if the underlying hardware adjusted the timeout
             period.

